# st-checkpoint.sh exclude file — one pattern per line, then '#' and a JUSTIFICATION.
#
# RULES, each of which cost something when it was broken:
#
#   * ANCHOR every pattern with a leading '/'. An unanchored pattern matches by NAME
#     anywhere in the tree. `--exclude='target/'` looks like "skip build output" and on
#     the real run it would have dropped `_research/re/target/`, which was the ground-truth
#     ORACLE BINARY DROP DIR, not cargo output.
#   * JUSTIFY every pattern against an actual listing of that directory — not against what
#     its name suggests. `--exclude='_research/re/rust/'` was meant to skip a cargo target
#     dir; it dropped 26 files / 1.44 MB of irreplaceable source including the keystone
#     `fasp-core/src/cap.rs`. The correct pattern was `/_research/re/rust/target/`.
#   * READ THE ACCOUNTING st-checkpoint.sh prints. Every pattern comes back with a file and
#     byte count. An over-broad exclude must show up as a NUMBER, never as silence.
#   * A pattern that removes 0 files is also worth noticing: it usually means you guessed
#     the path wrong and something you meant to skip is still coming along.
#
# Supported syntax (a documented subset of rsync's, implemented identically by st_walk.py
# so the accounting and the transfer cannot disagree):
#   /a/b/        anchored directory
#   /a/b         anchored path of any type
#   name/        any directory component called `name`, anywhere   (UNANCHORED)
#   *.pyc        fnmatch against any path component                (UNANCHORED)
# '**' is rejected: rsync's meaning for it is not reproduced here.
#
# An UNANCHORED pattern must be declared as such, with the `unanchored:` prefix. That is
# the whole point: it makes "this matches by name everywhere" a decision you typed, not a
# default you inherited.

# ---- examples. DELETE THESE and write your own against a real listing. ----

/rust/target/                            # cargo build output; verified by `ls`: only *.rlib/*.o/build/
/node_modules/                           # npm install output, regenerable from package-lock.json
unanchored:__pycache__/                  # bytecode caches, regenerable, appear at every depth
unanchored:*.pyc                         # bytecode, regenerable
unanchored:.pytest_cache/                # test runner cache
unanchored:.DS_Store                     # macOS Finder junk

# ---- patterns that look right and are NOT. Kept here as a warning. ----
# /_research/re/rust/                    # WRONG: this is source, not build output
# unanchored:target/                     # WRONG: matched the oracle binary drop dir too
